WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.868
Pressure changes how fast gases mix.

2
00:00:03.251 --> 00:00:10.159
At lower pressure, gas molecules are further apart, so they collide less often and spread out faster.

3
00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:14.640
Halve the pressure, and gases spread about twice as fast.

4
00:00:15.151 --> 00:00:20.608
On Earth, rising hot air does most of the mixing, so this barely matters.

5
00:00:21.038 --> 00:00:28.108
In microgravity, spreading is the only way oxygen reaches a flame, so it matters a great deal.

6
00:00:28.793 --> 00:00:35.167
At low pressure, oxygen arrives from further away. The flame grows larger, and dimmer.

7
00:00:35.863 --> 00:00:43.920
A Moon Base would run at about 10.2 psi, well below the space station's 14.7.

8
00:00:44.362 --> 00:00:50.735
So the evidence that counts comes from tests at the right pressure, not only the right oxygen percent.

9
00:00:51.432 --> 00:00:57.203
NASA's database holds 33 microgravity tests in the Moon Base range.

10
00:00:57.713 --> 00:01:04.970
All of them burned fuel droplets. None tested a gas flame or a cabin material in that atmosphere.
